    "summary": "This PR adds a complete Fortran-to-Rust conversion pipeline (interactive CLI, BLAS fetcher, Fortran parser/call graph, LLM client, three conversion strategies, numerical accuracy test harness, benchmarker, HTML reporter) together with a devcontainer, requirements and README documentation. Almost all of it is new code, so reviewers should focus on fortran_to_rust/test_harness.py, which auto-generates the Fortran drivers and Rust example binaries that decide whether a conversion is numerically correct and it is the component that gates every later milestone (dgemm, 10 functions, full BLAS). The generated drivers/examples contain several type and output-ordering mismatches that can make the accuracy verdict wrong (function results compared against array elements, single-precision routines represented as f64/double, non-real arrays omitted entirely).",
    "risk_level": "high",
    "rule_findings": [],
    "suggestions": [
      {
        "file_path": "fortran_to_rust/test_harness.py",
        "line": 557,
        "severity": "high",
        "confidence": 0.65,
        "title": "Function result printed before array outputs, while the Fortran driver prints it after",
        "reason": "The Rust FUNCTION template prints the return value first: line 557 `    println!(\"{{:.15e}}\", _result as f64);` comes before `{rust_prints}` (line 560). In `generate_fortran_driver` the opposite happens: the array WRITE loops are appended to `print_lines` at lines 418-441, and the return value is appended afterwards at line 471 (`print_lines.append(f\"      WRITE(*,'{print_fmt}') {result_var}\")`, comment: `# Print the function result after all array prints`). The two output streams therefore have different element order.",
        "recommendation": "Make both sides emit the return value in the same position, e.g. in the FN template move the result print after the array prints (`{rust_prints}` then the `println!`), or in `generate_fortran_driver` build the result write into a separate list and concatenate it *before* `print_lines` when `routine_kind == \"function\"`.",
        "source": "ai",
        "evidence": [
          "    println!(\"{{:.15e}}\", _result as f64);"
        ],
        "failure_scenario": "Converting `DOUBLE PRECISION FUNCTION DDOT(N,DX,DY)` produces a Fortran driver that writes DX(1..4), DY(1..4) and then RES, while the Rust example prints the dot product and then DX(1..4), DY(1..4). A positional comparison of the two streams pairs the scalar result with DX(1) and reports a huge max error, so a correct conversion is reported as failing (and a wrong conversion can accidentally cancel out)."
      }
    ],
